- unreagent-worker: helpers.go, tools.go, scripts.go and tests.
- Then unreagent-skill-author: unreagent-build and unreagent-runtimes, on the reply shape and what to do (a narrower re-run, or a user command that writes a file plus read_file).
- It runs after k180, because both touch tools.go.

unreagent-worker: started on cf77ca8 (worktree branch worktree-agent-a4a7d126a33bcf2f3). Readings I am taking, mine, not the scope's: (1) the head is the start of the whole output, not of the 300-line window. One marker between head and tail can only count every dropped line if nothing is dropped above the head, so the 300-line window does not apply to a failing reply; the 10,000 bytes bound it, and a failing output that fits shows whole even past 300 lines. (2) capReply does not cut whole lines only: it also cuts into the oldest line it keeps. The scope says whole lines, so the new cut drops whole lines and cuts bytes only from a single line longer than its share (the first line for the head, the last for the tail). (3) Where a line is cut, the text left out starts or ends inside a line, so a line count would be wrong; the marker then counts that span's bytes with the existing byte marker, its own reason ('a failing call's reply holds at most 10 KB'), never both markers. Plain lines keep '[... N lines omitted]' with no reason, as the spec writes it. (4) 'Under 10,000 bytes' implemented as at most 10,000 (the test item's 'never exceeds').

